= scx Debian packaging - build instructions (README.source)

This git repository contains ONLY the C<debian/> directory; the upstream
source lives in a separate directory (C<scx-1.1.3> in this workspace).
The packaging directory is dropped into an unpacked copy of the upstream
source and the package is built with the standard debhelper/dpkg
toolchain.  Rust crates are fetched during the build by
C<debian/fetch-vendor.sh> (the builder needs outbound network access for
this step, as granted on the Better-Deepin OBS instance); the actual
compilation afterwards is fully offline (see "Rust dependency sourcing").

== Quick build (amd64, builder with network access) ==

Run from the root of this git repository (which holds C<debian/>):

    SRC=/path/to/scx-1.1.3            # upstream source
    OUT=/tmp/scx-build; mkdir -p $OUT
    rm -rf $OUT/scx-1.1.3
    cp -a "$SRC" $OUT/scx-1.1.3
    cp -a debian $OUT/scx-1.1.3/
    cd $OUT/scx-1.1.3
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc         # or: debuild / pdebuild / sbuild

The resulting .deb files are written to $OUT:
I<scx-scheds_1.1.3-1_amd64.deb>, I<scxtop_1.1.3-1_amd64.deb> and their
I<*-dbgsym> companions.

C<3.0 (quilt)> means C<dpkg-source> wants a matching
C<scx_1.1.3.orig.tar.gz> next to the tree (the upstream release
C<.tar.gz> from GitHub renamed accordingly, or C<git archive>).  If you
only need the binary packages, C<dpkg-buildpackage -d> skips the
genbuild step and no orig tarball is required at all.

== Rust dependency sourcing ==

B<No librust-*-dev packages are used at all.> C<debian/control> only
carries the C<Rust toolchain> itself (C<cargo>, C<rustc>,
C<libstd-rust-dev>) plus the non-Rust libraries; the crates themselves
are fetched from U<https://crates.io> at build time by a dedicated
scripted step:

=over

B<1. Pre-fetch (the only network step).>
L<execute_before_dh_auto_configure|file:///debian/rules> runs
C<debian/fetch-vendor.sh>, which executes C<cargo vendor
--versioned-dirs vendor> for the quilt-patched workspace (the member
pruning keeps the closure minimal and equal to the build graph, ~496
crates for 1.1.3).  This requires outbound network access, which the
Better-Deepin OBS builder explicitly permits.

B<2. Offline build.> C<debian/cargo-config.toml> is rendered (with
C<@VENDOR_DIR@> substituted) into C<$CARGO_HOME/config.toml>, replacing
the crates-io source with ./vendor and setting C<net.offline = true>.
The C<cargo build> that follows therefore compiles strictly from the
just-vendored snapshot - no registry index, no implicit downloads.  The
upstream project's own C<.cargo/config.toml> (xtask alias) is kept and
untouched; CARGO_HOME level configuration overrides nothing of it.

B<3. No Built-Using.> Because no crate comes from a Debian crate
package, the L<Built-Using> / L<Static-Built-Using> fields are absent;
the exact crate versions are pinned by L<Cargo.lock> and mirrored by the
C<.cargo-checksum.json> files inside ./vendor.

=back

== Build-dependends ==

The build needs: C<debhelper-compat (= 13)>, C<cargo>, C<rustc> (>= 1.88:
C<time>, C<sscanf> and C<darling> in Cargo.lock declare rust-version
1.88, and the lockfile is v4), C<libstd-rust-dev>,
C<clang> (>= 16, the scx BPF build refuses older clang; on deepin v25
the B-D is satisfied by the C<clang> metapackage which pulls in
C<clang>-21/C<clang>-22),
C<libclang-dev> (bindgen), C<libelf-dev>, C<zlib1g-dev> (vendored libbpf
inside C<libbpf-sys>), C<libseccomp-dev> (C<seccomp> crate for
C<scx_rustland_core>) and C<pkg-config>.

No upstream C/C++ build system (C<lib/Makefile>) is invoked; schedulers,
their BPF objects and bindings are all produced by the C<cargo build>
build scripts.

== Excluded workspace members ==

By default C<xtask> (release helper), C<vmlinux_docify> (build-time doc
tool), C<scx_forge_agent> (optional scheduler-management web agent, pulls
reqwest/openssl-sys/aws-lc-sys + cmake) and C<scx_mitosis> (pulls the
same transitive stack through ktstr/gix) are removed from the workspace
members by C<debian/patches/prune-unshipped-members.patch>, so they are
neither built nor vendored and C<cmake> / C<libssl-dev> are not required
on the build host.  To include them, drop that patch from
C<debian/patches/series>, add the extra binaries to the install lists in
C<debian/rules>; the crate closure grows automatically on the next
vendor step.  C<cmake> and C<libssl-dev> must then also be installed,
and the build takes noticeably longer.

== Tests ==

C<cargo test> is skipped: the upstream test suites load BPF programs
into the running kernel and cannot pass on build daemons.  A CLI smoke
test is shipped as an autopkgtest (C<debian/tests/control>).

== Cross-building / architectures ==

C<debian/rules> passes C<--target $(DEB_HOST_RUST_TYPE)> explicitly to
cargo (C<include /usr/share/rustc/architecture.mk> provides the triple);
cross toolchains (C<clang>, C<libclang-dev> of the foreign arch) are
needed.  loongarch64 is
deliberately absent from the C<Architecture:> lists because
L<rust/scx_utils/vmlinux.tar.zst> only ships x86/arm/arm64/mips/powerpc/
riscv/s390 BTF headers, so building BPF objects is impossible there.

== Upstream version updates ==

Refresh C<debian/changelog> (upstream version in the first column) and
rename/unpack the source accordingly; C<debian/watch> (version 5, GitHub
template) downloads the new release for C<uscan>.  The crate closure is
re-vendored by C<debian/rules> automatically on the next build
(C<./debian/fetch-vendor.sh> can also be run manually once in the
combined tree to warm up or inspect it); nothing is vendored in this git
repository and no extra orig tarball is to be distributed.
